    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "I mean, I do believe the Ukrainians can win in the sense of expelling Russians from their territory, but they do need the US to be with them financially. And understand that there are a couple of reasons for that. There's the obvious, the U.S., largest accounting in the world, providing economic assistance is crucial, but also is signal sending. When we stand with Ukrainians and Moldovans, as they're being pummeled in the case of Ukraine by a brutal authoritarian neighbor, we're sending a signal that the free world will stand together and that will push back against authoritarianism. That signal is terrifically important if the U.S. were to back away. if the u.s. were to go quiet not only is there an obvious immediate financial need we send a terrible signal of this give you one quick story john something that i'll never forget i visited st. michaels cathedral one of the last stops i had a new crane in st michaels is that iconic bright blue church that you see over the shoulder of every news reporter that report from key beautiful golden dome and i was there ten years ago So I went to revisit the church, and I ran into a soldier. I ran into a young guy in uniform. And when he saw me, he came up to me, we were talking, pulled up his sleeve, showed me his bullet wounds from the defensive buccia, where the terrible atrocities took place. And he looked at me, and he simply said, you're American. Are you with us? And I said, I think so. And he breathed this deep sigh of relief. So much of the world looks to the U.S. I know Americans get tired of hearing about obligations and the need to engage and the need to lead. But looking in the eyes of this soldier, looking in the eyes of a woman that I met who lost her husband to a missile attack, America is a force for good and the irreplaceable force for good in the world.",
